#!/bin/sh
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 DGR Operations <repo@dagric.com>
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
# Dagric OS — stop shipping a gzip initramfs that nobody chose.
#
# /etc/initramfs-tools/initramfs.conf in this image says COMPRESS=zstd. That is
# Debian's own default in Trixie, and it has never once taken effect: the
# shipped initrd.img is gzip. mkinitramfs decides like this, in its own source
# at /usr/sbin/mkinitramfs:
#
#     if ! command -v "${compress}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
#             echo "W: No ${compress} in ${PATH}, using gzip" >&2
#             compress=gzip
#     fi
#
# ...and /usr/bin/zstd is not in the chroot. The reason is worth writing down,
# because it is not a forgotten line in a package list. initramfs-tools-core
# carries `Recommends: zstd, busybox | busybox-static`, and auto/config builds
# with --apt-recommends false. So the recommendation is dropped, mkinitramfs
# prints one warning into a fifteen-minute build log that nobody reads, and
# every boot pays gzip. Nothing in the tree is wrong; two correct decisions
# just cancel each other out silently.
#
# MEASURED, on 2 cores (which is what the target hardware has), compressing
# this image's real 264 MB initramfs cpio:
#
#     gzip      7 s   109.1 MB      <- what ships today
#     zstd -9   2 s    88.4 MB      <- Debian's default, what this hook gets
#     zstd -19 44 s    81.4 MB
#
# zstd -9 is strictly better than gzip on both axes: it is faster to produce
# AND 20.7 MB smaller. There is no trade being made here, which is rare enough
# to be worth saying out loud.
#
# WHY THE SIZE MATTERS MORE THAN THE DECOMPRESSION. On the live ISO, GRUB reads
# the whole initrd through UEFI block IO before the kernel gets control, at
# roughly 13 MB/s — measured, by screenshotting the boot every second: the
# black rectangle GRUB punches through the splash lasts 19 s on the shipped
# image, and the kernel only takes over for the last ~3 s of it. Every megabyte
# removed from the initrd is a megabyte of that rectangle. In-kernel
# decompression is the smaller half: 0.77 s of gzip against roughly 0.3 s of
# zstd.
#
# COMPRESSLEVEL IS DELIBERATELY LEFT AT DEBIAN'S 9. Level 19 buys 7.1 MB more,
# about half a second of GRUB read, and it was not distinguishable from level 9
# in an end-to-end boot. It costs 44 s instead of 2 s on two cores — and
# mkinitramfs does not only run here, it runs on the owner's laptop at every
# kernel update. Trading forty extra seconds of a stalled `apt upgrade` on an
# eight-year-old machine for half a second of boot is the wrong way round. No
# conf.d file is shipped for this: initramfs.conf already says what we want,
# and restating a setting that is already correct is how a config directory
# turns into a pile of guesses.
#
# THE OTHER HALF, NOW APPLIED. The squashfs is the bigger half by far, and it
# is one flag, which auto/config now sets:
#
#     lb config --chroot-squashfs-compression-type zstd
#
# live-build defaults to `-comp xz` when that flag is unset (binary_rootfs, the
# `# Set squashfs compression type or default to xz` branch), so the image
# decompresses xz on every single file read while the Plasma session starts —
# which is where most of the boot actually goes. auto/config also sets
# --chroot-squashfs-compression-level 19, which is an ISO-size decision and not
# a boot-speed one; see the table below and the note in auto/config.
#
# Measured, power-on to Plasma wallpaper, UEFI/OVMF under QEMU-KVM, ISO on SSD,
# host page cache dropped before every run, five variants run round-robin for
# three rounds, median:
#
#     shipped: xz squashfs + gzip initrd                    69 s
#     zstd initrd only (this hook)                          67 s
#     zstd squashfs only                                    63 s
#     both                                                  56 s
#
# Read that table the honest way: this hook ALONE is worth about 2 s, which is
# barely outside the run-to-run spread. Its real job is to make the squashfs
# change cheap — zstd costs the squashfs 115 MB, and the initrd gives 21 MB of
# it straight back, twice over, because the live ISO carries the initrd both at
# /live/initrd.img and again inside the squashfs at /boot.
#
# The block size and compression level are worth knowing about and are NOT
# worth reaching for on boot-time grounds; measured on identical content:
#
#     xz    128K  (what ships)   1,924,943,872   122 s to build
#     zstd  128K                 2,040,176,640    75 s
#     zstd  256K                 1,995,149,312    90 s
#     zstd  256K  level 19       1,978,392,576   172 s
#
# zstd at the default level is 47 s FASTER to build than the xz it replaces.
# 256K blocks and level 19 buy back 62 MB of the 115 MB and made no difference
# to boot time (55 s and 54 s against 56 s, all inside the spread), so treat
# them as an image-size decision, not a speed one. Note there is no
# `lb config` flag for block size — only --chroot-squashfs-compression-type and
# --chroot-squashfs-compression-level exist. mksquashfs picks it up from
# MKSQUASHFS_OPTIONS in lb build's environment, which works but is undocumented
# passthrough rather than a supported knob.
#
# VERIFIED END TO END, not just reasoned about. This hook was run inside a real
# chroot unpacked from the shipped squashfs; it produced a 140,674,044-byte
# initrd where gzip had produced 161,343,265. That exact initrd was put back
# into the ISO alongside a zstd squashfs and booted: 56 s to the desktop under
# OVMF against 69 s for the shipped image, and 49 s under SeaBIOS against 61 s.
# The desktop and the first-run wizard were opened and looked at in both, so
# "it booted" means somebody saw it, not that a timer expired quietly.
set -e


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one package that was missing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `zstd` now lives in config/package-lists/system.list.chroot, which is where a
# package belongs; this hook used to apt-get it because the change that found
# the bug was not allowed to edit the lists. The install is gone, the check is
# not, because the failure it catches is silent at every layer: with no zstd
# binary, mkinitramfs prints one line and produces a gzip initramfs, exiting 0.
# That is true on the owner's own disk at every future kernel update too, not
# just here.
#
# Not fatal. A gzip initramfs boots; it is just slower and 21 MB bigger, and
# failing a fifteen-minute build over it would be the wrong call.
command -v zstd >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    echo "WARNING: zstd is not installed. It is listed in" >&2
    echo "         config/package-lists/system.list.chroot — if it is still" >&2
    echo "         there, apt dropped it; if it is not, someone removed it." >&2
    echo "         The initramfs below will be gzip." >&2
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Regenerate, and check that it actually took
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0700-plymouth.hook.chroot also runs update-initramfs, later, so the shipped
# initrd would end up zstd even if this hook only installed the package. It is
# regenerated here anyway, for two reasons: so this file is correct on its own
# rather than by depending on the internals of a hook three hundred lines away,
# and so the build log says whether zstd took at the moment we can still do
# something about it. The cost is one extra initramfs build per image.
BEFORE=$(stat -c%s /boot/initrd.img-* 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head -1)

# mkinitramfs announces the gzip fallback on stderr and then exits successfully,
# so the exit status tells us nothing and the message is the whole signal.
# Capture both streams and read it back.
OUT=$(update-initramfs -u 2>&1) || {
    printf '%s\n' "$OUT" >&2
    echo "WARNING: update-initramfs failed — shipping whatever initrd was there" >&2
}
printf '%s\n' "$OUT"

AFTER=$(stat -c%s /boot/initrd.img-* 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head -1)

case "$OUT" in
    *"using gzip"*)
        echo "WARNING: mkinitramfs fell back to gzip despite COMPRESS=zstd." >&2
        echo "         That is the exact failure this hook exists to fix, so" >&2
        echo "         something undid it: check that zstd installed, and that" >&2
        echo "         CONFIG_RD_ZSTD=y in /boot/config-*." >&2
        ;;
    *)
        # Not a proof that the container is zstd — nothing cheap in the chroot
        # can parse a multi-segment initrd — but it is the exact inverse of the
        # documented failure, and the size is the corroborating evidence.
        #
        # The size is reported, not asserted on, with one exception. Asserting
        # "it must have shrunk" was the first version of this and it was wrong:
        # on any build where the initramfs was ALREADY zstd — a rebuild, or the
        # day zstd moves into the package list — it regenerates at the same size
        # and the check fired a warning at a completely correct image. A test
        # that fails on a good build teaches people to ignore the build log.
        # Growing by more than a percent is the thing that would actually mean
        # something (gzip is ~15% larger than zstd here), so that is all we
        # check for.
        if [ -n "$BEFORE" ] && [ -n "$AFTER" ]; then
            echo "boot-speed: initramfs $BEFORE -> $AFTER bytes"
            if [ "$AFTER" -gt $((BEFORE + BEFORE / 100)) ]; then
                echo "WARNING: the initramfs GREW by more than 1%. Going from" >&2
                echo "         zstd back to gzip looks like this; check for a" >&2
                echo "         second COMPRESS= under /etc/initramfs-tools/conf.d." >&2
            fi
        fi
        ;;
esac


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REJECTED — measured, and deliberately not shipped.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPRESSLEVEL=19 for the initramfs
#   See the header. 7.1 MB for 42 extra seconds of every kernel update on the
#   owner's machine, and no boot-time difference outside the noise.
#
# MODULES=dep instead of MODULES=most
#   The single biggest thing in the initrd, and it has to stay. `dep` includes
#   only the modules the machine BUILDING the image needs; a live ISO exists to
#   boot hardware nobody has seen. It would also take the GPU drivers out of
#   the initramfs, which is what lets KMS come up before the root switch and is
#   the whole basis of the flicker-free work.
#
# Pruning firmware out of the initramfs
#   Tempting: of the 264 MB uncompressed initramfs, 37.3 MB is kernel modules
#   and most of the rest is firmware, and a live boot demonstrably does not need
#   Mellanox NIC firmware before it has mounted the squashfs. Rejected because
#   the failure mode is unbootable, not slow: get the allow-list wrong for one
#   storage controller and that machine never reaches the desktop, and it will
#   be somebody else's machine, not ours. If this is ever revisited it needs a
#   deny-list of provably-not-storage firmware, not an allow-list, and a boot
#   test on real hardware.
#
# Disabling units to reach the display manager sooner
#   The critical chain to sddm.service was read out of a kmsg-on-serial log of
#   a real boot: live-config (2.7 s) -> firewalld (2.1 s) -> network-pre.target
#   -> NetworkManager -> network.target -> systemd-user-sessions -> sddm. Both
#   costs are real and both are things the product sells; 0250-performance
#   already refuses to disable firewalld and gives the reason. Nothing else on
#   that chain is over a second. systemd reaches graphical.target in 12.2 s of
#   a 69 s boot, so the units are not where the time is and no drop-in was
#   shipped under /etc/systemd/system — there was nothing to buy.
#
# NetworkManager-wait-online
#   Already disabled by 0250-performance.hook.chroot, and verified absent from
#   the built chroot's network-online.target.wants. The classic answer to this
#   question was already done; re-doing it would just have looked like progress.
